<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.claude-fleet.authprobe</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@HOME@/.local/share/claude-fleet/scripts/fleet-probe-auth</string>
    <string>--quiet</string>
  </array>
  <!-- Hourly, and enabled by default, for a reason written into the script it
       runs: credentials expired on 2026-07-17 and were not noticed until 07-29.
       Eleven days looking entirely green. A long-lived daemon keeps the old
       token in memory, so everything already running kept working while every
       NEW process died — which is why nothing in-process could see it. The
       check has to spawn.

       `fleet-health` treats a stale probe as DEGRADED, so shipping this
       disabled would mean every fresh install is degraded out of the box, and a
       check that is red on a healthy machine is a check people learn to ignore.

       It does not refresh anything — refresh rotates the refresh token, and two
       hosts racing on that has already cost this fleet four accounts. It asks
       whether a fresh process can complete one trivial exchange, on two paths
       that fail independently (bare credentials, and through the launcher).
       Two trivial exchanges an hour against a subscription. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>3600</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>@HOME@/.local/state/claude-fleet/authprobe.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>@HOME@/.local/state/claude-fleet/authprobe.error.log</string>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
